dan Anaya. Oncolnc: linking tcga survival data to mrnas, mirnas, and lncrnas. PeerJ Computer Science , 2:e67, 2016. 1\n- [4] Bilal Aslam, Madiha Basit, Muhammad Atif Nisar, Mohsin Khurshid, and Muhammad Hidayat Rasool. Proteomics: technologies and their applications. Journal of chromatographic science , pages 1-15, 2016. 2\n- [5] Rita Canas-Marques and Stuart J Schnitt. E-cadherin immunohistochemistry in breast pathology: uses and pitfalls. Histopathology , 68(1):57-69, 2016. 2\n- [6] Sean B Carroll. [truncated]","original_chars":18071},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Datasets and Evaluation Metrics","heading_level":3,"line_start":171,"line_end":180,"text":"We use five cancer datasets from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) to evaluate HFGPI: Bladder Urothelial Carcinoma (BLCA) ( n = 340 ), Breast Invasive Carcinoma (BRCA) ( n = 833 ), Glioblastoma and Lower Grade Glioma (GBMLGG) ( n = 878 ), Lung Adenocarcinoma (LUAD) ( n = 368 ), and Uterine Corpus Endometrial Carcinoma (UCEC) ( n = 122 ). These cohorts are selected based on the availability of matched genomic, proteomic and pathology imaging data, enabling multimodal analysis. Genomic Data. Gene expression profiles are obtained from the UCSC Xena database [16], originally generated using the Illumina HiSeq 2000 RNA-Seq platform. Expression values are RSEM normalized and log 2 (x+1) transformed to stabilize variance and reduce the impact of outliers. We retain genes with sufficient expression variability across samples for downstream analysis.\n\nProteomic Data. Protein expression data are acquired from Reverse Phase Protein Array (RPPA) measurements curated by UCSC Xena [16]. These datasets provide unit-normalized expression values for cancer-related proteins and phosphoproteins, quantifying functional proteomic states that serve as intermediate phenotypes between genomic alterations and histopathological manifestations.\n\nPathology Images. The diagnostic WSIs are collected from the National Cancer Institute (NCI) Genomic Data Commons (GDC) [56].\n\nEvaluation Metric. We employ 5-fold cross-validation with the 4:1 ratio of training and validation sets. The model performance is measured by the mean concordance index (C-index) [17] with its standard deviation (std).","original_chars":1581},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Method","heading_level":2,"line_start":39,"line_end":168,"text":"### 3.1. Overview\n\nFig. 1 illustrates the overview of HFGPI. Given a patient's WSIs, genomic data and proteomic data, our framework proceeds through four stages that reflect the biological hierarchy from genes to proteins to tissue morphology. First, we extract patch-level features from WSIs using a pre-trained vision encoder, and encode molecular data i.e., genes and proteins, through our proposed Molecular Tokenizer, which integrates identity embeddings with expression profiles (Sec. 3.3). Second, we introduce Gene-Regulated Protein Fusion (GRPF) to model geneprotein regulatory relationships through graph-aware crossattention with structure-preserving alignment, generating gene-regulated protein representations (Sec. 3.4). Third, Protein-Guided Hypergraph Learning (PGHL) constructs a hypergraph where each protein defines a hyperedge connecting semantically related image patches, and applies hypergraph convolution to capture higher-order proteinmorphology relationships (Sec. 3.5). Finally, we aggregate the hierarchically fused features through a Transformer encoder and attention-based pooling for survival prediction (Sec. 3.6).\n\n### 3.2. Problem Formulation\n\nFor the k -th patient, the available data is represented as I ( k ) = ( Y ( k ) , X ( k ) g , X ( k ) p , c ( k ) , t ( k ) ) , where Y ( k ) denotes WSI patch features, X ( k ) g and X ( k ) p are genomic and proteomic embeddings, c ( k ) ∈ { 0 , 1 } indicates the right uncensorship status, and t ( k ) ∈ R + is the overall survival time. [truncated]","original_chars":12515},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Comparisons with State-of-the-Art Methods","heading_level":3,"line_start":187,"line_end":220,"text":"To demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method, we compare it against state-of-the-art unimodal and multimodal survival prediction models. For genomic and proteomic data, we employ Self-Normalizing Network (SNN) [29] to predict survival outcomes from the original gene or protein expression profiles. For histopathology, we compare with state-of-the-art MIL methods, including ABMIL [21], CLAM [34], TransMIL [47], and WiKG [31]. For multimodal integration of histopathological images with genomic data, we compare with several recent proposed methods such as MCAT [9], MOTCat [60], CMTA [70], SurvPath [23], MMP [48], PIBD [68], and MoME [58]. In addition, we compare with methods capable of handling three modalities, including PS3 [43] and ICFNet [66], where we adapt them by replacing their original text modality with proteomic data to align with our experimental setup. We summarize the key findings below.\n\nUnimodal vs. Multimodal. Tab. 1 presents performance comparisons across five TCGA cohorts. Unimodal approaches achieve limited prognostic results, with genomic/proteomic methods averaging C-index of 0.677 and 0.647, while histopathology-only methods reach 0.686. In contrast, multimodal integration consistently outperforms unimodal baselines. Our HFGPI achieves an average Cindex of 0.753, significantly surpassing the best unimodal method i.e., WiKG, by 6.7%. This substantial gain suggests that heterogeneous modalities capture complementary biological aspects essential for accurate survival prediction.\n\nProtein vs. No Protein. [truncated]","original_chars":5508},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Baseline Methods","heading_level":2,"line_start":418,"line_end":497,"text":"### 8.1. Uni-modal Baselines\n\nSNN [29]: Self-Normalizing Networks employ scaled exponential linear units (SELUs) to enable self-normalization properties, which we apply to gene and protein expression profiles for survival prediction.\n\nABMIL [21]: ABMIL uses an attention mechanism to aggregate patch-level features from whole slide images, enabling the model to focus on diagnostically relevant regions for survival prediction.\n\nCLAM [34]: CLAM incorporates instance-level clustering to identify distinct morphological patterns and uses attention-based aggregation for slide-level survival prediction.\n\nTransMIL [47]: TransMIL employs self-attention mechanisms to capture long-range dependencies among image patches, enabling more effective context-aware feature aggregation for histopathology-based survival analysis.\n\nWiKG [31]: WiKG constructs dynamic instance graphs to model spatial relationships among tissue regions and employs graph neural networks for survival prediction from histopathological images.\n\n### 8.2. Two-modality Baselines\n\nMCAT [9]: MCAT employs cross-modal attention to learn interpretable, dense co-attention mappings between WSI features and genomic profiles within a shared embedding space.\n\nMOTCat [60]: MOTCat employs optimal transport to establish soft correspondences between genomic data and image patches, providing global awareness to capture structural interactions within the tumor microenvironment. [truncated]","original_chars":8433}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_003954.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003954","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a new HFGPI framework, not a review or wrapper, and it centers genomic, proteomic, and histology data. The selected sections also show natural-language protein descriptions encoded by a text encoder and generated with LLMs, so the text-bio bridge is substantive.","evidence_snippet":"“We propose HFGPI, a hierarchical fusion framework”; “protein descriptions (text encoder)”; “LLMs for generating protein descriptions”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003954","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"HFGPI is presented as a hierarchical fusion framework for cancer survival prediction, not a generative text-bio model. The only generative wording is auxiliary LLM use for protein descriptions; the core model is a predictive fusion pipeline using external pretrained encoders.","evidence_snippet":"“Transformer encoder and attention-based pooling for survival prediction”; “LLMs for generating protein descriptions”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC3_not_generative","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]

